    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A public adjuster is a licensed claims professional you hire to prepare and negotiate your property insurance claim. The adjuster your insurance company sends works for the carrier. We work only for you. TX Public Adjusting is run by Joseph Dittman, Texas Public Adjuster License #3356839, and we handle residential and commercial claims in every Texas county from our base in Belton.</p>
<p>On a house, that usually means a roof, a kitchen, or a whole structure after a fire. On a commercial property it can mean multiple buildings, a TPO or metal roof system, business personal property, and a tenant list. The work is the same either way: find all of the covered damage, price it accurately, and back the number with evidence the carrier has to respond to.</p>

<h2>Claims we handle</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="/services/roof-hail-damage-claims/">Roof and hail damage claims</a>: test squares, soft-metal hits, cosmetic exclusions, ACV roof endorsements, and the roof components a carrier estimate tends to leave off.</li>
<li><a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">Water damage claims</a>: burst pipes, supply-line and slab leaks, storm leaks, mitigation invoices, and the "sudden and accidental" question.</li>
<li><a href="/services/fire-smoke-damage-claims/">Fire and smoke damage claims</a>: structure, contents inventories, smoke in the HVAC system, debris removal, and living expenses while you are displaced.</li>
<li><a href="/services/hurricane-windstorm-claims/">Hurricane and windstorm claims</a>: TWIA and private carriers, percentage deductibles, and the wind-versus-flood causation dispute.</li>
<li><a href="/services/denied-underpaid-claims/">Denied and underpaid claims</a>: reading the denial letter, re-inspections, supplements, appraisal, and knowing when a lawyer is the better fit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Business owners can also see how we approach <a href="/commercial-claims/">commercial property claims</a> by building type.</p>

<h2>How a claim works with us</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Free claim review.</strong> Call or text us. We look at your policy declarations, any estimate or letter you already have, and photos, and tell you plainly whether we think we can help.</li>
<li><strong>On-site inspection and documentation.</strong> We inspect in person with roof-level or drone photos, Matterport 3D scans of interiors, and moisture meters on water losses.</li>
<li><strong>Xactimate estimate.</strong> We write a line-item estimate in Xactimate, the same software most carriers use, so every difference from their number is visible line by line.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation.</strong> We submit the estimate and supporting documents and handle the back-and-forth with the carrier's adjuster.</li>
<li><strong>Re-inspections and supplements.</strong> We attend re-inspections with the carrier and file supplements when hidden damage turns up during repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal if needed.</strong> If the dispute is over the amount of loss and your policy has an appraisal clause, we advise you on invoking it.</li>
<li><strong>Settlement.</strong> You approve the final number. Checks are made out to you, and we never sign or endorse them.</li>
</ol>

[[cta]]

<h2>What it costs</h2>
<p>Our fee is a contingency: a percentage of the claim payment. There is no upfront fee and the claim review is free. If there is no recovery, there is no fee.</p>
<div class="callout callout--law"><p><strong>Texas law:</strong> A public adjuster's total commission may not exceed 10% of the settlement (Tex. Ins. Code §4102.104). Our contract is on a TDI-approved form, states that we represent the insured only, and lets you cancel within 72 hours of signing (§4102.103).</p></div>
<p>We also don't repair property on claims we adjust, and we take no money from contractors or salvage companies. Texas law prohibits both (§4102.158). For a fuller explanation, read <a href="/post/the-truth-about-public-adjuster-fees/">how public adjuster fees work</a>.</p>

[[tool:do-i-need-a-public-adjuster]]

<h2>When to call</h2>
<p>The best time is before you accept a final number, but it is rarely too late while the claim is open. People usually call us when the carrier's estimate is far below a contractor's bid, when a letter says the damage is "wear and tear" or "cosmetic", when a loss is too large to manage alongside work and family, or when the claim has stalled for weeks. If you are unsure, our guide on <a href="/post/should-i-use-a-public-adjuster/">whether to use a public adjuster</a> covers the cases where you probably don't need one.</p>
